### What is the Rod Cutting problem?

Given a rod of length N and a price list where price[i] is the revenue for selling a piece of length i, cut the rod into pieces to maximize total revenue. Each length can be used any number of times. This is a classic dynamic programming problem that maps directly to the Unbounded Knapsack pattern.

```java
package com.bank.investment;

public class RodCutting {

    public static int maxRevenue(int[] prices, int rodLength) {
        int[] dp = new int[rodLength + 1];
        // dp[0] = 0: rod of length 0 yields 0 revenue

        for (int len = 1; len <= prices.length; len++) {
            for (int c = len; c <= rodLength; c++) {
                dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], prices[len - 1] + dp[c - len]);
            }
        }
        return dp[rodLength];
    }
}
```

### Why is this an Unbounded Knapsack problem?

In the Unbounded Knapsack, you have a capacity (the rod length), items with weight and value (each cut length with its length as weight and its price as value), and each item can be used unlimited times. The rod can contain multiple pieces of the same length, which is the defining characteristic of the unbounded variant. The upward sweep from len to N is what enables reuse of the same length.

```java
// Unbounded Knapsack mapping:
// Capacity    = rod length N
// Items       = available cut lengths 1, 2, ..., N
// Item weight = cut length
// Item value  = price for that length
// Reuse       = same length can appear multiple times (upward sweep)
```

### How does the upward sweep enable reuse?

In the 0/1 Knapsack, you sweep capacity downward so each item is used at most once. In the Unbounded Knapsack, you sweep upward. When computing dp[c], dp[c - len] may already include a piece of the same length from this iteration, effectively allowing that length to be used again.

```java
package com.bank.investment;

public class UpwardSweepDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        int[] prices = {2, 5, 9}; // lengths 1, 2, 3
        int rodLength = 6;
        int[] dp = new int[7]; // dp[0..6]

        // Processing length 3 (price 9):
        // dp[3] = max(dp[3], 9 + dp[0]) = 9
        // dp[6] = max(dp[6], 9 + dp[3]) = 9 + 9 = 18
        // dp[3] was updated earlier in THIS sweep, so length 3 is reused!
        // Rod of length 6 = two pieces of length 3, revenue = 18

        System.out.println("Max revenue for rod 6: " + RodCutting.maxRevenue(prices, rodLength));
    }
}
```

```text
Max revenue for rod 6: 18
```

### How do you reconstruct the actual cuts?

To know which lengths were chosen, maintain a parallel array that records which length improved each capacity. After computing dp[N], trace back from N by subtracting the recorded length at each step until you reach zero.

```java
package com.bank.investment;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class RodCuttingWithPlan {

    public static List<Integer> maxRevenueWithCuts(int[] prices, int rodLength) {
        int[] dp = new int[rodLength + 1];
        int[] cutUsed = new int[rodLength + 1]; // which length improved each capacity

        for (int len = 1; len <= prices.length; len++) {
            for (int c = len; c <= rodLength; c++) {
                int candidate = prices[len - 1] + dp[c - len];
                if (candidate > dp[c]) {
                    dp[c] = candidate;
                    cutUsed[c] = len;
                }
            }
        }

        // Reconstruct cuts
        List<Integer> cuts = new ArrayList<>();
        int remaining = rodLength;
        while (remaining > 0) {
            cuts.add(cutUsed[remaining]);
            remaining -= cutUsed[remaining];
        }

        System.out.println("Max revenue: " + dp[rodLength]);
        System.out.println("Cut plan: " + cuts);
        return cuts;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] prices = {2, 5, 9};
        maxRevenueWithCuts(prices, 6);
    }
}
```

```text
Max revenue: 18
Cut plan: [3, 3]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Aspect | Complexity | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(N * L) | N = rod length, L = number of available lengths |
| Space | O(N) | 1D DP array |
| With reconstruction | O(N) extra | Parallel cutUsed array |

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include a rod of length zero (revenue is zero), a single available length, and prices where selling the rod uncut is more profitable than any combination of cuts.

```java
package com.bank.investment;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        // Rod length 0: no cuts possible, revenue = 0
        System.out.println(RodCutting.maxRevenue(new int[]{5}, 0)); // 0

        // Single length: price[1]=3, rod=5 → 5 pieces of length 1 = 15
        System.out.println(RodCutting.maxRevenue(new int[]{3}, 5)); // 15

        // Uncut is best: prices = [1, 5, 6, 10], rod=4 → uncut length 4 = 10
        System.out.println(RodCutting.maxRevenue(new int[]{1, 5, 6, 10}, 4)); // 10
    }
}
```

```text
0
15
10
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is sweeping capacity downward (as in 0/1 Knapsack), which prevents reusing the same length. Another mistake is using a 0-indexed price array without adjusting for 1-indexed lengths.

```java
package com.bank.investment;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: downward sweep (0/1 Knapsack style) - prevents reuse
    public static int wrongVersion(int[] prices, int rodLength) {
        int[] dp = new int[rodLength + 1];
        for (int len = 1; len <= prices.length; len++) {
            for (int c = rodLength; c >= len; c--) { // BUG: downward sweep!
                dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], prices[len - 1] + dp[c - len]);
            }
        }
        return dp[rodLength]; // Returns suboptimal result: each length used at most once
    }

    // CORRECT: upward sweep allows reuse
    public static int correctVersion(int[] prices, int rodLength) {
        int[] dp = new int[rodLength + 1];
        for (int len = 1; len <= prices.length; len++) {
            for (int c = len; c <= rodLength; c++) { // upward sweep!
                dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], prices[len - 1] + dp[c - len]);
            }
        }
        return dp[rodLength];
    }
}
```

### How is this used in banking?

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh used this to split a fixed-term deposit into sub-terms to maximize total interest. Each sub-term length has a known interest rate, and the same sub-term can be reused as often as it fits within the total term. The DP finds the optimal split, and the cut reconstruction tells the customer exactly which sub-terms to choose.

```java
package com.bank.investment;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TermDepositOptimizer {

    public static void optimize() {
        // Sub-term interest rates (for lengths 1, 2, 3 years)
        // Length 1: 5% of principal
        // Length 2: 12% of principal
        // Length 3: 20% of principal
        int[] interestBasisPoints = {500, 1200, 2000};
        int totalTerm = 6; // 6 years

        // Rod Cutting: maximize total interest
        // Optimal: two 3-year sub-terms = 20% + 20% = 40%
        System.out.println("Optimal split for 6-year deposit:");
        RodCuttingWithPlan.maxRevenueWithCuts(interestBasisPoints, totalTerm);
    }
}
```

```text
Optimal split for 6-year deposit:
Max revenue: 4000
Cut plan: [3, 3]
```
